description:
In LegionCapitalRaise.publishRaisedCapital() (line 396-412) and LegionPreLiquidApprovedSale.publishRaisedCapital(), the totalCapitalRaised is set to the admin-provided `capitalRaised` parameter without on-chain verification against totalCapitalInvested:
  s_capitalRaiseStatus.totalCapitalRaised = capitalRaised;
Legion (onlyLegion) can set this to any value. If set to a value greater than the contract's actual bid token balance, withdrawRaisedCapital() will revert. If set to a value less than the actual balance, the difference is permanently locked. The fee calculations (legionFee, referrerFee) operate on this admin-set value. This creates a trust dependency on Legion admin accuracy, with no on-chain invariant enforcing capitalRaised == totalCapitalInvested - totalCapitalRefunded.

In LegionTokenDistributor.claimTokenAllocation() (line 164):
  s_tokenDistributorConfig.totalAmountClaimed += claimAmount;
The claimAmount is verified by signature (_verifyValidPosition) but there is no on-chain check that the cumulative totalAmountClaimed does not exceed totalAmountToDistribute. The signature-based authorization is the only guard. If Legion's signer produces duplicate or inconsistent signatures, or if a bug exists in the signature verification logic, an investor could claim more than their allocation, potentially draining the contract. The contract relies entirely on Legion signer integrity rather than maintaining a balance invariant.
